task: small-wins (the remainder of the operator's "take everything", minus JSON which is a product decision)
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/small-wins

parts, each measured or reasoned on its own line:
  1. Timer on the JVM: Timer.after starts a virtual thread that
     sleeps -- 5.5 KB per ask (its stack chunk), ~1ms + 40% over the
     asked sleep (channel-batch-floor's probe). A shared
     ScheduledExecutorService arms a timer as one small task and runs
     the callback on a virtual thread only when it FIRES; a cancelled
     timer (every fast ask) allocates nothing beyond the task.
     Measured on actorAsk (13.0us / 5.5 KB per ask) and the dwell
     probe's precision. Every Timer user gets it: ask, sleep, race.
  2. drainedChunks on Channel: the receiveMany batches emitted as
     Chunk[A], the right door for a caller who wants chunks from an
     element channel -- `.drained.chunked()` re-chunks what Drain
     already batched and read 318.7 against 209 elementwise (section
     6c). Measured against chunkNative; documented as the door, with
     `.chunked()` on a drained source named as the wrong one.
  3. Source.unfold's tuple per step (13% over range on its lane):
     read first; taken only if the fix is small.
  4. Drain.copy per element: DECLINED by design -- Stream.uncons
     answers Option[(A, S)], a fresh cursor per element is the
     re-observation law, and the copy is that cursor. Recorded.
  5. actor-stop-strands: a supervised Stop/Escalate closes the
     mailbox with accepted messages inside and `stopped` never comes
     true. Drain-and-discard after close so `finished` means what it
     says; the law in TestPoisonLaws asserts `stopped` again; the
     actor docs' "stop, DRAINING" line gets its qualifier.
